Draw the **Lattice curved transport belt**, an 8-frame **sprite sheet** of an
animated factory conveyor making a 90-degree turn, using only the drawing tool.

The curved belt is a renderer sprite for **Lattice**, a grid-based factory
simulation rendered in a high-angle, pseudo-3D style; the belt is its flat,
ground-level layer, drawn essentially from straight above. You are drawing one
**corner tile**: the same dark **single** continuous metal belt as the straight
transport belt — one belt with side rails and a central row of amber chevrons
painted on it, not two belts — only bent through a quarter turn. In the one
orientation you draw, flow **enters at the West edge travelling East** and **leaves
at the South edge travelling South**, curving clockwise between them. The belt's
surface carries **tread blades** across it as well as the amber chevrons, and the
**whole surface — blades and chevrons together — scrolls around the arc** across the
eight frames so the belt itself reads as moving and the animation loops seamlessly.
The renderer rotates and mirrors this one sprite for the other corners, so you draw
this West-in / South-out curve only.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. You
build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a time**, each targeting a frame
with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you issue are recorded to a per-frame
action log, and that log is your submission.

Each frame is its **own separate 32×32 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-circle --frame 0 --x 0 --y 31 --radius 32 --color "#34383d"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the curved belt sprite sheet up operation by operation until each
`frames/<index>.png` matches the brief: a dark single continuous belt surface — a
filled quarter-round that runs into the sharp inner corner so both mouths stay full
width — sweeping a 90-degree arc from a full-width West entry face round to a
full-width South exit face, with one rail along the outer edge of the curve, tread
blades patterned across the surface, and a central row of amber chevrons that follow
the arc from East-pointing at the entry to South-pointing at the exit — the whole
surface pattern (blades and chevrons) scrolling forward along the curve by the same
step each frame so the belt reads as moving and frames 0→7 loop seamlessly. When you
are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-frame action logs are your finished
submission.
